# T33235 — (3) Port — Fixed port speed — tb470 IE520 stack — 2026-09-29 — PARTIAL (stopped by Terrence)

Verdict: PARTIAL. Run 1 (12:17:39–12:57:03, framework rc = 3 after SIGINT) ran 12 of the script's
30 TestCases, then Terrence stopped it: the bench has no fibre link between the DUT and a partner,
so the fibre sweep (cases 8–12) graded `!!FAIL: … not applicable` one after another and each FAIL
cost a full-bench PDU power cycle — "its useless" without the fibre link. He will cable fibre
(stack port1.0.25 <-> IE520-sa port1.0.25) and the case re-runs on fixed scripts.
- TestCase_1..7 PASS — copper speed sweep on stack member 1 port1.0.9 (AT-SPTXc copper SFP) <->
  x230-10GP port1.0.4: baseline auto a-full/a-1000; `speed 10` REJECTED by the DUT
  (`% port1.0.9: Unsupported speed/duplex combination`); `speed 100` + `duplex full` ACCEPTED and
  linked `full 100`; `speed 1000` + `duplex full` ACCEPTED and linked `full 1000`; 2500, 5000 and
  10000 REJECTED (both ends), link unchanged at a-1000, no boot output, console at the prompt.
- TestCase_8..12 FAIL = SCRIPT GRADING, not a product result: the frame declared the fibre role
  OPTIONAL at init ("the cases needing it report UNSUPPORTED", 12:17:48) but each fibre case
  graded `!!FAIL: no fibre test link fitted …; case not applicable`, and the framework then
  power-cycled stk_a/swi_b/swi_e/swi_f five times (12:33:20, 12:39:08, 12:43:54, 12:49:31,
  12:55:08). Test-cases 3454bc0 ("the fibre sweep cases are excluded before they run on a bench
  with no fibre link") is the fix; nothing measured for these cases.
- TestCase_13..30 not run (fixed speed S, invalid arguments, shutdown/no shutdown, mismatch,
  monitored ports, copy run start, reload).
Run labelled: clean for cases 1–7 (the copper readings stand); confounded for 8–12 — because the
bench has no fibre link and the script graded that as FAIL.

Operator: bench-runner subagent (device-testing-61 = sentinel, /test-mode shape A). The first
tester subagent (gate, isolation, launch, cases 1–12) was killed mid-run by a user interrupt; a
second bench-runner took the bench over at 12:55 for the stop, restore and record. Terrence at
the bench.

